[Music]  kabali National Park is in western  Uganda  and it was a forest preserved for quite  a while before it became a national park  was actually established as a forest  reserve in 1932 by the British colonial  authorities in 1995 if it officially  became a national park chimpanzees are  interesting as chimpanzees but they do  have a privileged position when it comes  to looking at humans and trying to  understand the origins of ourselves and  of our own behavior over the years I've  done focused research on aspects of  social relationships especially those of  males which are really interesting and  important in chimpanzees males are  fascinating they are really unusual for  mammals and in ways that are rather like  male humans males are more sociable than  females and they spend just more time  with other adult chimpanzees and  especially with each other and they they  are rivals they compete they compete for  mating opportunities they just they  compete for status which then can  influence mating opportunities but  there's a lot of friendly interaction  that goes on between males and male  chimpanzees groom with each other more  than they groom with females and more  than females groom with each other and  that's the exact opposite of what you  would see in a baboon group for example  males also cooperate with each other in  other ways they form some of them form  alliances with each other they will back  each other up against others in their  community and that can be extremely  important in males efforts to rise in  rank and the dominance hierarchy and  especially can be really important for a  male who becomes alpha he may not get  there unless he has effective alliances  and if he gets there he may not be able  to quite likely won't be a  stay there and whisk in unless he has  some allies who will back him up against  other males on another level though all  the males in a community are allies with  each other against males in neighboring  communities and again one of the  somewhat disturbingly human-like aspects  of chimpanzee behavior is that  neighboring groups don't get along in  fact they are intensely hostile to each  other and most of the hostility is males  against outsiders and it takes the form  of groups of males in engaging in  aggression towards outsiders and if they  meet a group of outsiders and they're  sort of evenly matched then they may  make a lot of noise but they're unlikely  to try to get engaged in any serious  fights if it's 15 from n go go and only  one or only a few from the neighboring  community then they try to attack them  and then it's all the males from in Go  Go cooperating in this and making a gang  attack really that can be extremely  serious to the point where they kill  other chimpanzees a number of Yale  graduate students who have done PhD work  there and some of them have also  incorporated laboratory work into their  project so they go there and do  fieldwork and they go around following  chimpanzees and collect urine from them  for example and then they can do  hormonal asses back here and you can get  information on endocrinology you can get  genetics data and then that allows us to  really to answer questions that people  have wanted to have answers to but not  been able to but if we can get enough  good data on genetics then we can tell  who the fathers are which you could  never do from behavioural data given the  chimpanzee mating system which is  complicated but most of the mating that  is most of the copulations between males  and females take place in situations  where there's a female who's interested  in may  and she's with a number of males  sometimes especially in gogo  many sexually mature males and she may  actually mate with all of them we just  can't know who the fathers are unless we  can do paternity determinations using  DNA which is possible now and we have  well over 70 paternity determinations  that's an extraordinary number to have  that many now and this is an ongoing  project now we know who a lot of the  fathers are we can see if there's  anything unusual about relationships  between know that male and his actual  offspring compared to his relationship  say to other infants or juveniles  mothers and their offspring can have  really lifelong social ties with each  other although for the most part that's  mothers and their sons we have one  wonderful old lady who has to be in her  50s now and she has a son who himself is  getting rather old now he's definitely  middle-aged he's our best guess is he's  in his late 30s they still spend a lot  of time together they groom with each  other especially he grooms her a lot  he's he's a he's a good son he's still  very close with his mother there's a lot  of variation in the social worlds of  females and at an gogo one important  finding we've made is that the females  there are more sociable than females in  most other chimpanzee populations and  that's because they've got a better food  supply generally and given that better  availability of food they can just  afford to be with each other more and  some of them have long-term social bonds  with each other and in a few cases we've  discovered or we know that these are  mothers and daughters and the daughters  have not emigrated but in other cases we  know they aren't related to each other  but they've developed real friendships  with each other and these clearly can  last for years and years  chimpanzees are omnivores and they they  mostly eat fruit and the leaves and  plant stems and flowers and in some  places but not really didn't go go they  some insects they like to eat meat and  they eat more meat than any other  primates except humans and they get it  by hunting and usually hunts involve  groups of chimpanzees who go after  groups of monkeys and wherever  chimpanzees live with red colobus  monkeys they focus on them and they  especially hunt them they don't eat meat  every day they might go for weeks  without any and some individuals go for  longer than that but they really like it  and it makes sense it's not easy to get  but it's nutritionally complete food  essentially it's it's very valuable  nutritionally even small bits of it  could be valuable to them so first of  all they get wildly excited while they  are hunting and then when they have meat  and someone has some others are really  excited then they want some and they  share me I often see things like one  male has a monkey and he will bite off a  leg and literally hand it to someone  else who's likely to be his know his  ally or his number-one grooming partner  this is again it's highly unusual  behavior compared to other mammals but  rather like what humans do um the just  the willingness to share and the extent  to which everyone can benefit when they  have something valuable like this a  couple of years ago one of my students  from Yale and I were out one day and we  found a big group of chimpanzees a big  party as we call them um out in the  western part of the in gogo chimps  territory and there was a lot of  excitement when we got there many  chimpanzees together lots of good food  um and we thought oh where this is going  to be a good day we're gonna see a lot  of interesting things but then we  realized that really there was a lot of  excitement  down the hill from where we were in this  little valley we got down there and we  found that the chimpanzees had  encountered alliance snares that  poachers had set Quentin who was then a  young an adolescent female who had  pretty recently joined the in Go Go  community had been caught in a snare  she'd managed to break away from the  pole but she had this length of she had  a the noose of wire pulled very tightly  around her hand and she had which and  she was trailing this wire and it had  gotten caught up in some vegetation and  she was desperately trying to free  herself but she was she didn't know how  to do that so she was pulling and  pulling and just making things worse and  and the other chimpanzees were very  upset by this and several came right up  to her even touch her and gently  reassuringly they didn't know how to get  her free so I think they were showing a  kind of a real concern  [Music]  we pay several men who are experts at  anti-poaching work and they work with  Park Rangers they help train them they  also work on their own sometimes and  they do a great job and they really  contribute importantly to protection of  the chimpanzees and their habitat but  they can only cover a small fraction of  that habitat and they could find more  men whom they could train who would also  do a good job and the Ugandan Park  authorities have told us that they would  be happy to have us hire more men to  cover more of the park and the only  question is at the moment we don't have  the money to do that if we did then we  could really step up the protection of  the chimpanzees well there's a world out  there that's somewhat parallel tum' to  mine as a an academic and a researcher  and that's the world of people who make  wildlife documentaries alistair  Fothergill who was the producer for the  chimpanzee film the disney nature film  he was the one who really came up with  the idea of doing a feature film and he  also approached John Mitani my colleague  collaborator and me and said this was  what he was trying to do he wanted to  make this film would we be amenable to  working with him and having the film  crew come to a go-go and so on this is  not a documentary unlike the BBC films  but it is just chimpanzees and it's all  footage of wild chimpanzees and he  wanted to have a film crew come to a  go-go because he knew that that's the  place to film wild chimpanzees I had two  roles in the film process and one was  that they asked me to look at scripts  and if I said oh well you know this  makes no sense whatsoever this would  never happen  um they were willing to listen  that and change it a bit usually what I  did was I went out early while they were  getting ready getting their equipment  packed up and so on and I had a radio so  I could call to them back in camp and  say you know I'm out it you know each  10:00 and I've got a good group here so  you should head out this way then I  would help follow the chimps help them  keep up with them during the day the  cameraman bill wall hour who is an  expert wildlife cinematographer also  knows chimpanzees because he worked for  the Jane Goodall Institute at Gombe for  many years I could certainly give him a  lot of background information about oh  this is miles he's the alpha male now  and this is his history and this is  what's going on between him and monk  who's over there and who's sort of his  ally now they understood that it's  really important to have that context to  know what to look for and try to film in  the first place and then to make sense  out of the images that they got when  they know the context and in know the  history and they know even who they're  looking at and what those individuals  are like and what their relationships  with each other are it will have a lot  of good information about chimpanzees  and it will that will go along with just  amazing images wonderful photography and  I hope it inspires even more people to  understand that these are very  complicated animals and they are  fascinating and wonderful and also that  their their status is rather shaky now  they're in trouble in the wild and they  need attention and they need help from  people it is overwhelmingly clear now  that the presence of long term research  projects helps to protect habitats and  helps to protect the animals we did  stand to learn a lot about ourselves and  about human evolutionary history  certainly by looking at chimpanzees  and there are some obvious places to  look and just at hunting and meat  sharing for example or a male aggression  towards outsiders these are things that  are characteristics of humans  [Music]  you

